WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:02.673
- When people tell us
something we don't want to hear

00:00:02.673 --> 00:00:06.420
our first reaction is they're
wrong and they're confused.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.467
Sometimes it's hard to look
in that mirror and say,

00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:10.163
"You know what, maybe I'm wrong."

00:00:10.163 --> 00:00:12.046
Do you ever go online and you're
going to look up something

00:00:12.046 --> 00:00:13.519
it's going to take three minutes.

00:00:13.519 --> 00:00:15.069
Three hours later you're still online

00:00:15.069 --> 00:00:16.202
you don't know why you went online

00:00:16.202 --> 00:00:17.035
in the first place.

00:00:17.035 --> 00:00:21.192
The Great Western disease
is I will be happy when.

00:00:21.192 --> 00:00:24.010
When I get the money, the status, the BMW,

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:27.720
the condominium, when I
get this I will be happy.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.250
- He's an American
leadership coach and author

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:32.299
of several management related books.

00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:33.709
He's been recognized as one of the

00:00:33.709 --> 00:00:35.704
top leadership thinkers in the world.

00:00:35.704 --> 00:00:37.505
He's the author of 35 books

00:00:37.505 --> 00:00:40.661
which have sold over two
million copies worldwide.

00:00:40.661 --> 00:00:41.968
He's Marshall Goldsmith

00:00:41.968 --> 00:00:44.602
and here's my take on his
top 10 rules for success.

00:00:44.602 --> 00:00:46.330
Rule number three is my personal favorite

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:47.640
I make sure to stick around all

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.585
the way to the end for
some special bonus clips.

00:00:49.585 --> 00:00:51.149
And as always if Marshall says something

00:00:51.149 --> 00:00:52.501
that really resonates with you

00:00:52.501 --> 00:00:53.833
leave it in the comments below

00:00:53.833 --> 00:00:55.823
as he's saying it and put quotes around it

00:00:55.823 --> 00:00:59.156
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:01:01.891 --> 00:01:04.474
(gentle music)

00:01:11.291 --> 00:01:14.228
- It's very easy to see what we don't like

00:01:14.228 --> 00:01:16.895
about ourselves in other people.

00:01:18.404 --> 00:01:21.253
It's a little harder to
see what we don't like

00:01:21.253 --> 00:01:23.753
about ourselves in the mirror.

00:01:24.702 --> 00:01:28.407
And sometimes other people
can see things in us

00:01:28.407 --> 00:01:30.657
that we don't want to see in ourselves.

00:01:30.657 --> 00:01:32.839
And when that happens
our first reaction is,

00:01:32.839 --> 00:01:35.208
you're wrong, you're confused.

00:01:35.208 --> 00:01:37.312
What people tell us something
we don't want to hear

00:01:37.312 --> 00:01:40.839
our first reaction is, they're
wrong and they're confused.

00:01:40.839 --> 00:01:43.011
Sometimes it's hard to
look in that mirror and say

00:01:43.011 --> 00:01:45.001
you know what, maybe I'm wrong.

00:01:45.001 --> 00:01:46.626
And maybe I'm confused.

00:01:46.626 --> 00:01:48.094
What did I learn?

00:01:48.094 --> 00:01:50.639
Sometimes other people
can see something in us

00:01:50.639 --> 00:01:52.163
we can't see in ourselves

00:01:52.163 --> 00:01:54.712
and our first reaction
is they're confused.

00:01:54.712 --> 00:01:58.235
A great challenge learn
to listen to other people.

00:01:58.235 --> 00:02:00.182
Hear what they have to say and

00:02:00.182 --> 00:02:03.597
recognize sometimes they
can see something in us

00:02:03.597 --> 00:02:05.337
that we don't want to see in ourselves.

00:02:05.337 --> 00:02:07.686
We have something called the monkey mind.

00:02:07.686 --> 00:02:09.962
Our monkey mind is our
mind is in many ways like a

00:02:09.962 --> 00:02:12.432
monkey swinging through
the vines in the jungle.

00:02:12.432 --> 00:02:14.623
You know thing ding ding ding
ding ding doing ding ding.

00:02:14.623 --> 00:02:16.803
Going from this thing to thing
and the Internet provides

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:19.073
amphetamines for the monkey mind.

00:02:19.073 --> 00:02:20.813
You know do you ever go
online you're going to

00:02:20.813 --> 00:02:22.704
look up something it's
going to take three minutes.

00:02:22.704 --> 00:02:24.095
Three hours later you're still online.

00:02:24.095 --> 00:02:26.107
You don't know why you want
online in the first place.

00:02:26.107 --> 00:02:28.902
Monkey mind me me me me me me me me.

00:02:28.902 --> 00:02:30.705
It's very hard to kind

00:02:30.705 --> 00:02:33.079
of calm down the monkey mind.

00:02:33.079 --> 00:02:35.829
- Do you have concrete techniques

00:02:36.929 --> 00:02:38.364
for calming the monkey mind?

00:02:38.364 --> 00:02:40.271
- Yeah the more you measure things

00:02:40.271 --> 00:02:42.521
the less likely you are
to have this happen.

00:02:42.521 --> 00:02:44.762
And also the more we admit we need help

00:02:44.762 --> 00:02:45.978
in structure.

00:02:45.978 --> 00:02:48.354
When you get over that macho
I can do everything on my own

00:02:48.354 --> 00:02:51.001
nonsense, life is better.

00:02:51.001 --> 00:02:53.683
So if you know look
I'm going to go online.

00:02:53.683 --> 00:02:55.919
Let's look at my history here.

00:02:55.919 --> 00:02:58.137
I'm going to set the alarm clock off

00:02:58.137 --> 00:02:59.852
so in five minutes this alarm clock

00:02:59.852 --> 00:03:01.372
is going to go off and I'm not going

00:03:01.372 --> 00:03:03.209
to be online anymore.

00:03:03.209 --> 00:03:05.773
You know you might say
that sounds childish well

00:03:05.773 --> 00:03:07.674
if you don't do it guess what?

00:03:07.674 --> 00:03:08.928
Shhhhhhhhhhhhhh.

00:03:08.928 --> 00:03:13.447
The Great Western disease
is I will be happy when.

00:03:13.447 --> 00:03:16.113
When I get the money, the status, the BMW,

00:03:16.113 --> 00:03:17.248
the condominium.

00:03:17.248 --> 00:03:20.450
When I get this I will be happy.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:23.230
Well this is the Great Western message

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:25.065
and it's supported by what I call

00:03:25.065 --> 00:03:27.403
the Great Western art form.

00:03:27.403 --> 00:03:29.173
Now what is the Great Western art form?

00:03:29.173 --> 00:03:31.775
This is something you've seen in your life

00:03:31.775 --> 00:03:33.440
probably hundreds of thousands

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:35.315
perhaps millions of times.

00:03:35.315 --> 00:03:37.168
It's always the same story,

00:03:37.168 --> 00:03:39.238
the story sounds like this.

00:03:39.238 --> 00:03:41.288
There is a person.

00:03:41.288 --> 00:03:42.749
The person is sad.

00:03:42.749 --> 00:03:46.916
They spend money to buy
product and they become happy.

00:03:47.989 --> 00:03:50.644
This is called a commercial.

00:03:50.644 --> 00:03:53.527
Have you ever seen one of those before?

00:03:53.527 --> 00:03:56.169
The message is always the same.

00:03:56.169 --> 00:03:59.709
It's not in here, it's out there.

00:03:59.709 --> 00:04:01.944
I will be happy when.

00:04:01.944 --> 00:04:04.407
The reality is lottery
winners have been studied

00:04:04.407 --> 00:04:07.348
to death and when someone wins the lottery

00:04:07.348 --> 00:04:09.076
within three years statistically

00:04:09.076 --> 00:04:11.932
they're not much happier
than they were to start with.

00:04:11.932 --> 00:04:13.368
What's been learned as happiness

00:04:13.368 --> 00:04:14.949
doesn't come from the outside.

00:04:14.949 --> 00:04:17.381
Most of happiness comes from the inside.

00:04:17.381 --> 00:04:19.408
Well the great employee engagement myth

00:04:19.408 --> 00:04:22.204
is the employees will be engaged when.

00:04:22.204 --> 00:04:24.166
When we give them this program.

00:04:24.166 --> 00:04:28.011
When we do this process
they will be engaged when.

00:04:28.011 --> 00:04:29.793
Well that's partially true I mean

00:04:29.793 --> 00:04:31.903
better leaders lead to more engagement

00:04:31.903 --> 00:04:35.166
and better recognition
leads to more engagement.

00:04:35.166 --> 00:04:37.681
The reality is also though a lot of

00:04:37.681 --> 00:04:40.906
employee engagement doesn't
come from the outside.

00:04:40.906 --> 00:04:43.970
A lot of employee engagement
comes from the inside.

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:46.550
And one of the things that
I'm going to talk about

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:49.934
is how do you create an
environment where people take

00:04:49.934 --> 00:04:52.970
responsibility for their own engagement

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:55.554
and instead of focusing
on I will be engaged

00:04:55.554 --> 00:04:59.589
when the company does something
for me take responsibility

00:04:59.589 --> 00:05:04.585
for their own engagement
and say I can engage myself.

00:05:04.585 --> 00:05:05.418
Lying less

00:05:06.447 --> 00:05:07.280
do more.

00:05:09.322 --> 00:05:12.029
We spend an inordinate
amount of time in life

00:05:12.029 --> 00:05:16.477
whining, typically whining
about decision makers.

00:05:16.477 --> 00:05:18.710
Whining about decisions.

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:22.225
I'm going to talk about
five of my favorite whines

00:05:22.225 --> 00:05:24.016
and then we'll talk about how many times

00:05:24.016 --> 00:05:24.980
you've heard these.

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:26.141
And then we'll look in the mirror and say

00:05:26.141 --> 00:05:27.861
how many times have I done it myself?

00:05:27.861 --> 00:05:31.278
That first whine is called it's not fair.

00:05:32.381 --> 00:05:33.548
It's not fair.

00:05:34.564 --> 00:05:37.453
The decision maker makes
the decision we conclude

00:05:37.453 --> 00:05:38.620
it's not fair.

00:05:39.625 --> 00:05:42.625
Decisions have nothing
to do with fairness.

00:05:42.625 --> 00:05:44.428
Every decision is made by the person

00:05:44.428 --> 00:05:46.139
who has a power to make the decision.

00:05:46.139 --> 00:05:48.556
Not a fair person, right
person or logical person.

00:05:48.556 --> 00:05:50.496
It's made by that person.

00:05:50.496 --> 00:05:52.390
The decision maker is the customer

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:54.798
and our job is to influence
the decision maker.

00:05:54.798 --> 00:05:56.785
Not to just judge other people.

00:05:56.785 --> 00:05:59.667
As Peter Drucker said our job in life

00:05:59.667 --> 00:06:01.075
is to make a positive difference

00:06:01.075 --> 00:06:02.372
not prove we're right.

00:06:02.372 --> 00:06:03.618
Well it's not fair.

00:06:03.618 --> 00:06:05.133
The kid comes home from school.

00:06:05.133 --> 00:06:08.439
Daddy, mommy I got a C on my report card.

00:06:08.439 --> 00:06:10.081
I should have gotten an A.

00:06:10.081 --> 00:06:11.579
It's not fair.

00:06:11.579 --> 00:06:14.489
A great learning experience for the child

00:06:14.489 --> 00:06:17.502
decisions aren't made based on fairness.

00:06:17.502 --> 00:06:19.922
You did not sell the teacher.

00:06:19.922 --> 00:06:22.007
The teacher was the decision maker.

00:06:22.007 --> 00:06:24.925
The teacher thought you
should have gotten a C.

00:06:24.925 --> 00:06:26.798
You thought you should have laid an A.

00:06:26.798 --> 00:06:27.938
Well you don't grade your own paper

00:06:27.938 --> 00:06:29.388
the teacher graded the paper.

00:06:29.388 --> 00:06:30.268
You got a C.

00:06:30.268 --> 00:06:33.657
There's always a teacher,
there's always a boss,

00:06:33.657 --> 00:06:37.188
there's always a customer,
there's always somebody

00:06:37.188 --> 00:06:39.438
and it's never really fair.

00:06:40.473 --> 00:06:43.071
Another common whine is it's not smart.

00:06:43.071 --> 00:06:45.172
Yeah they're doing that and they think

00:06:45.172 --> 00:06:46.864
it's fair but it's not smart.

00:06:46.864 --> 00:06:48.842
I have a much better idea
and the world would be

00:06:48.842 --> 00:06:51.021
better if they did it my way.

00:06:51.021 --> 00:06:53.979
Well sometimes the world doesn't ask us

00:06:53.979 --> 00:06:55.830
how we want to do things.

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:58.007
And we've got to do things that others do

00:06:58.007 --> 00:07:00.291
and maybe their definition of smart is

00:07:00.291 --> 00:07:02.315
a little bit different than than our

00:07:02.315 --> 00:07:04.374
definition of smart.

00:07:04.374 --> 00:07:06.099
And a favorite among engineers

00:07:06.099 --> 00:07:07.949
that's related to these two is

00:07:07.949 --> 00:07:10.081
it's not logical.

00:07:10.081 --> 00:07:11.792
People with technical backgrounds

00:07:11.792 --> 00:07:14.283
scientists, engineers constantly look for

00:07:14.283 --> 00:07:16.226
logic where none exists.

00:07:16.226 --> 00:07:18.605
And then we're shocked
because we can't find it.

00:07:18.605 --> 00:07:20.493
Now I have a degree in
mathematical economics

00:07:20.493 --> 00:07:21.703
I went to an engineering school so I

00:07:21.703 --> 00:07:24.025
include myself in this category.

00:07:24.025 --> 00:07:26.018
Too much of my life has
been wasted looking for

00:07:26.018 --> 00:07:27.911
logic where none exists

00:07:27.911 --> 00:07:29.688
and being shocked when I can't find it.

00:07:29.688 --> 00:07:32.457
Well decisions are not
always made based on logic.

00:07:32.457 --> 00:07:34.625
Oftentimes crazy people make decisions

00:07:34.625 --> 00:07:36.611
and it's not logical.

00:07:36.611 --> 00:07:39.828
Sometimes we say well it
they think it's logical

00:07:39.828 --> 00:07:42.139
and there they try to
be fair it's just the

00:07:42.139 --> 00:07:43.380
wrong decision.

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:46.765
They mean well, it's just not right,

00:07:46.765 --> 00:07:48.678
it's not right.

00:07:48.678 --> 00:07:52.200
Well again we don't get to
necessarily as we wander

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:55.387
through life decide what is or isn't right

00:07:55.387 --> 00:07:57.544
in our definition of right

00:07:57.544 --> 00:07:59.575
what might not be somebody else's.

00:07:59.575 --> 00:08:02.668
Another big favorite is it's not their job

00:08:02.668 --> 00:08:03.972
it's my job.

00:08:03.972 --> 00:08:07.321
It's not their job, it's my job.

00:08:07.321 --> 00:08:10.042
Believe it or not I see
this with a lot of CEOs

00:08:10.042 --> 00:08:10.880
I coach.

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:12.540
Shockingly, I worked with a CEO

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:14.283
of a private equity firm.

00:08:14.283 --> 00:08:17.642
Private equity they own the company.

00:08:17.642 --> 00:08:19.773
I was hired by the owners.

00:08:19.773 --> 00:08:21.215
You know what he said?

00:08:21.215 --> 00:08:23.088
They can't tell me what to do.

00:08:23.088 --> 00:08:24.567
It's not their job.

00:08:24.567 --> 00:08:25.567
I'm the CEO.

00:08:26.412 --> 00:08:29.473
Shockingly you'd be surprised
how many people become

00:08:29.473 --> 00:08:31.684
braindead when they become CEOs.

00:08:31.684 --> 00:08:32.536
I'm the CEO.

00:08:32.536 --> 00:08:34.660
They can't tell me what to do.

00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:38.577
I had to sit down and
say they own the company.

00:08:39.863 --> 00:08:41.280
It's their money.

00:08:42.563 --> 00:08:45.979
They can indeed tell you what to do.

00:08:45.979 --> 00:08:48.062
You see you work for them

00:08:49.168 --> 00:08:52.039
they do not work for you.

00:08:52.039 --> 00:08:54.539
I had another CEO had the
same problem in a publicly

00:08:54.539 --> 00:08:56.530
traded company with his board.

00:08:56.530 --> 00:08:59.265
I had to sit down and say excuse me

00:08:59.265 --> 00:09:02.253
the Board of Directors
rightly or wrongly represents

00:09:02.253 --> 00:09:05.088
the ownership of the company.

00:09:05.088 --> 00:09:07.283
The board does not report to you

00:09:07.283 --> 00:09:10.000
you actually report to the board.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.794
Now fortunately in most cases as a coach

00:09:12.794 --> 00:09:14.182
I've been successful.

00:09:14.182 --> 00:09:15.613
I've had a failure.

00:09:15.613 --> 00:09:17.224
I had a failure where
a person never really

00:09:17.224 --> 00:09:18.462
quite got it.

00:09:18.462 --> 00:09:20.422
Now as we look at decision making

00:09:20.422 --> 00:09:21.652
realize one thing,

00:09:21.652 --> 00:09:24.040
our job is to make a positive difference.

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:26.901
Not to go through life
critiquing everybody else

00:09:26.901 --> 00:09:29.343
with our infinite wisdom and whining.

00:09:29.343 --> 00:09:31.083
And again some of the favorite whines

00:09:31.083 --> 00:09:32.756
we've talked about:

00:09:32.756 --> 00:09:34.378
it's not right, it's not fair,

00:09:34.378 --> 00:09:36.563
it's not smart, it's not logical,

00:09:36.563 --> 00:09:38.083
it's not their job,

00:09:38.083 --> 00:09:40.902
we can go through life
doing this all we want to

00:09:40.902 --> 00:09:42.481
What are we accomplishing?

00:09:42.481 --> 00:09:43.924
Nothing.

00:09:43.924 --> 00:09:46.502
Simple guideline for life

00:09:46.502 --> 00:09:48.782
do more, whine less.

00:09:48.782 --> 00:09:50.316
Take all the effort you might have spent

00:09:50.316 --> 00:09:53.373
on whining and complaining
about other people

00:09:53.373 --> 00:09:55.914
and focus on making a positive difference.

00:09:55.914 --> 00:09:58.191
Try to influence those other people

00:09:58.191 --> 00:10:00.578
don't waste so much
time judging, critiquing

00:10:00.578 --> 00:10:01.736
and whining.

00:10:01.736 --> 00:10:03.624
So now I'm working with my daughter Kelly.

00:10:03.624 --> 00:10:05.279
She's a professor at the Kellogg School

00:10:05.279 --> 00:10:07.339
at Northwestern, marketing professor.

00:10:07.339 --> 00:10:09.773
She taught me this thing
called active questions

00:10:09.773 --> 00:10:13.033
which I'm going to recommend
to all your viewers.

00:10:13.033 --> 00:10:15.482
Active questions that all started with

00:10:15.482 --> 00:10:17.300
did I do my best too?

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:19.730
Because that's the one thing
we have total control over,

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:20.563
did I do my best?

00:10:20.563 --> 00:10:22.197
I can't blame you if I didn't do my best.

00:10:22.197 --> 00:10:23.747
Back to accountability that's something

00:10:23.747 --> 00:10:24.912
you can't hide from.
- Right.

00:10:24.912 --> 00:10:26.902
- So the six I recommend every day

00:10:26.902 --> 00:10:29.360
and there I do this daily
question process every day

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:30.627
these are now my first six.

00:10:30.627 --> 00:10:33.385
Number one, did I do my
best to set clear goals?

00:10:33.385 --> 00:10:35.933
(mumbling) did somebody
else set goals for me,

00:10:35.933 --> 00:10:37.400
did I do my bestest at my own goals?

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:39.516
Two, did I do my best
to make progress toward

00:10:39.516 --> 00:10:41.221
achieving my goals?

00:10:41.221 --> 00:10:44.391
Number three, did I do
my best to find meaning

00:10:44.391 --> 00:10:46.763
rather than saying, did
somebody else give me meaning

00:10:46.763 --> 00:10:48.984
or create meaning, did I do my best

00:10:48.984 --> 00:10:50.411
to create meaning myself?

00:10:50.411 --> 00:10:52.386
Number four, did I do my best to be happy?

00:10:52.386 --> 00:10:54.427
Rather than, did somebody
else make me happy,

00:10:54.427 --> 00:10:56.531
did I do my best to make myself happy?

00:10:56.531 --> 00:10:57.942
Then number five, did I do my best

00:10:57.942 --> 00:10:59.554
to build positive relationships?

00:10:59.554 --> 00:11:01.433
And then finally, did I do my best

00:11:01.433 --> 00:11:02.706
to be fully engaged?

00:11:02.706 --> 00:11:05.926
Every day challenge yourself
with those six questions.

00:11:05.926 --> 00:11:07.632
Great process a great process.

00:11:07.632 --> 00:11:09.451
And we have research on this from

00:11:09.451 --> 00:11:11.121
thousands of people if any of the

00:11:11.121 --> 00:11:12.927
viewers want to
participate in the research

00:11:12.927 --> 00:11:14.679
send me an email, marshall with two L's

00:11:14.679 --> 00:11:15.791
at marshallgoldsmith.com.

00:11:15.791 --> 00:11:16.990
I'll sign them up for it.

00:11:16.990 --> 00:11:18.307
Research is amazing.

00:11:18.307 --> 00:11:20.643
46% of the people say I
got better at everything

00:11:20.643 --> 00:11:21.873
10 days later.

00:11:21.873 --> 00:11:24.571
75% said I got better at four out of six.

00:11:24.571 --> 00:11:27.174
90 I think four percent said I got better

00:11:27.174 --> 00:11:28.603
at least one.

00:11:28.603 --> 00:11:30.718
Six percent stayed the same and zero point

00:11:30.718 --> 00:11:31.635
something percent get worse.

00:11:31.635 --> 00:11:35.399
It's just amazing why every
day this gets me to focus not

00:11:35.399 --> 00:11:37.949
on what I cannot change.

00:11:37.949 --> 00:11:40.206
It gets me to focus on what I can change.

00:11:40.206 --> 00:11:41.427
My favorite advice

00:11:41.427 --> 00:11:43.341
imagine you're 95.

00:11:43.341 --> 00:11:44.910
Everyone look up here

00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:46.496
take a deep breath and do your hand like

00:11:46.496 --> 00:11:48.905
this go (inhales) ahhhhh.

00:11:48.905 --> 00:11:50.322
Hand hand ahhhhh.

00:11:51.174 --> 00:11:52.388
Imagine your 95.

00:11:52.388 --> 00:11:54.824
And you're just getting ready to die.

00:11:54.824 --> 00:11:56.233
And you're on that deathbed.

00:11:56.233 --> 00:11:57.714
Here comes your last breath.

00:11:57.714 --> 00:11:59.054
Right before you take that breath

00:11:59.054 --> 00:12:00.471
you're given a beautiful gift.

00:12:00.471 --> 00:12:02.122
The ability to go back in time

00:12:02.122 --> 00:12:04.251
and talk to the person in this room.

00:12:04.251 --> 00:12:07.940
The ability to help this
person be a better leader.

00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:09.717
More important the ability
to help this person

00:12:09.717 --> 00:12:11.259
have a better life.

00:12:11.259 --> 00:12:13.499
What advice would the wise 95-year-old

00:12:13.499 --> 00:12:14.782
you who knows what mattered in life

00:12:14.782 --> 00:12:16.940
and what didn't have for the you

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:19.237
that's sitting here right now?

00:12:19.237 --> 00:12:21.717
You don't have to say
anything or do anything,

00:12:21.717 --> 00:12:24.304
just answer that question in your mind.

00:12:24.304 --> 00:12:27.276
Whatever you're thinking now do that.

00:12:27.276 --> 00:12:29.044
Terms say a performance appraisal.

00:12:29.044 --> 00:12:30.682
That's the only one's
that's going to matter.

00:12:30.682 --> 00:12:32.822
Some friends of mine interviewed
old folks who were dying

00:12:32.822 --> 00:12:34.067
and asking this question,

00:12:34.067 --> 00:12:36.519
what advice would you
have on the personal side

00:12:36.519 --> 00:12:37.459
three themes.

00:12:37.459 --> 00:12:39.366
Theme number one, three words,

00:12:39.366 --> 00:12:41.038
be happy now.

00:12:41.038 --> 00:12:43.901
Not next week, not next
year, not next month

00:12:43.901 --> 00:12:45.601
be happy now.

00:12:45.601 --> 00:12:47.726
Great Western disease, I'll be happy when.

00:12:47.726 --> 00:12:50.396
When I get the money, the status, the BMW.

00:12:50.396 --> 00:12:52.208
We all have the same when.

00:12:52.208 --> 00:12:54.038
That old person is when.

00:12:54.038 --> 00:12:56.423
Learning point from old
people I got so wrapped up

00:12:56.423 --> 00:12:58.138
chasing what I did not have

00:12:58.138 --> 00:12:59.972
I could not see what I did have

00:12:59.972 --> 00:13:01.931
and I had everything.

00:13:01.931 --> 00:13:04.676
People in this room many of
you are among the luckiest

00:13:04.676 --> 00:13:06.515
humans who ever lived.

00:13:06.515 --> 00:13:08.984
Many of you have friends
and family and health

00:13:08.984 --> 00:13:11.508
and you're doing fun work
and you're helping people.

00:13:11.508 --> 00:13:13.127
You got it all.

00:13:13.127 --> 00:13:15.542
Don't get so wrapped
chasing what you don't have

00:13:15.542 --> 00:13:17.000
you can't see it.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:19.139
Learning point number
two and this is point

00:13:19.139 --> 00:13:21.260
President Clinton made friends and family,

00:13:21.260 --> 00:13:24.536
when you're 95-years-old
and you look around that

00:13:24.536 --> 00:13:28.128
deathbed none of your
coworkers are waving goodbye.

00:13:28.128 --> 00:13:30.780
You realize these people
are kind of important.

00:13:30.780 --> 00:13:33.620
And learning point three, if
you have a dream go for it.

00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:35.905
Because if you don't go
for it when you're 35

00:13:35.905 --> 00:13:37.462
you may not when you're 45

00:13:37.462 --> 00:13:39.247
and you probably won't when you're 85.

00:13:39.247 --> 00:13:40.714
And it doesn't have to be a big one

00:13:40.714 --> 00:13:41.547
maybe a little one.

00:13:41.547 --> 00:13:43.549
Go to New Zealand, speak
Spanish, play a guitar.

00:13:43.549 --> 00:13:46.261
Other people think your
dream is goofy, who cares.

00:13:46.261 --> 00:13:48.041
It's not their dream, it's yours.

00:13:48.041 --> 00:13:50.205
It's not their life, it's your life.

00:13:50.205 --> 00:13:52.888
One of the greatest thinkers
in the history of leadership

00:13:52.888 --> 00:13:55.813
development is a hero of mine, a man named

00:13:55.813 --> 00:13:57.335
Dr. Warren Bennis.

00:13:57.335 --> 00:13:59.527
And Warren Bennis is a wonderful guy

00:13:59.527 --> 00:14:01.825
a guy who's helped me have a better life

00:14:01.825 --> 00:14:04.376
and someone I've always looked up to.

00:14:04.376 --> 00:14:07.485
Warren told the story once
I I'll always remember.

00:14:07.485 --> 00:14:10.003
For a while he was
president of the University

00:14:10.003 --> 00:14:11.016
of Cincinnati.

00:14:11.016 --> 00:14:13.638
And he's not sure he
wanted to be president

00:14:13.638 --> 00:14:15.004
for all the right reasons.

00:14:15.004 --> 00:14:17.191
He was president and he was giving a talk

00:14:17.191 --> 00:14:19.014
in front of a group of people

00:14:19.014 --> 00:14:21.727
and one gentlemen raised
his hand and he said,

00:14:21.727 --> 00:14:25.224
"Dr. Bennis, do you love what you do?

00:14:25.224 --> 00:14:27.649
"Do you love being president?"

00:14:27.649 --> 00:14:29.529
And Warren said he he stopped

00:14:29.529 --> 00:14:32.779
and there was almost a minute of silence.

00:14:32.779 --> 00:14:35.969
Eventually he looked at
this person and said,

00:14:35.969 --> 00:14:37.782
"I don't know."

00:14:37.782 --> 00:14:40.966
Well that moment kind
of changed his career.

00:14:40.966 --> 00:14:43.556
He realized maybe he
wasn't being the president

00:14:43.556 --> 00:14:44.969
for the right reasons.

00:14:44.969 --> 00:14:49.269
Things like status, ego, feeling important

00:14:49.269 --> 00:14:50.947
maybe those kicked in over things like

00:14:50.947 --> 00:14:52.699
doing what I love doing and

00:14:52.699 --> 00:14:54.145
doing what's in my heart.

00:14:54.145 --> 00:14:56.961
Well I thought a lot about
Warren Bennis example,

00:14:56.961 --> 00:14:58.935
do you love what you do?

00:14:58.935 --> 00:15:01.749
In the old days if you
didn't love what you do

00:15:01.749 --> 00:15:03.903
it wasn't so bad.

00:15:03.903 --> 00:15:08.605
For example, in 1979 IBM
was my biggest customer.

00:15:08.605 --> 00:15:12.130
IBM was the most admired
company in the world.

00:15:12.130 --> 00:15:14.022
They were the God company.

00:15:14.022 --> 00:15:16.907
In 1979 I could go to (mumbling) New York

00:15:16.907 --> 00:15:18.870
shoot a cannon ball down
the hall at five o'clock

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:20.328
and hit almost nobody.

00:15:20.328 --> 00:15:22.892
These people worked 35, 40 hours a week

00:15:22.892 --> 00:15:26.887
and they took five weeks of real vacation.

00:15:26.887 --> 00:15:30.044
I recently talked to the
chief Learning Officer at IBM,

00:15:30.044 --> 00:15:31.973
I said about those same
people in that same

00:15:31.973 --> 00:15:35.012
building now work 60 hours a week.

00:15:35.012 --> 00:15:37.711
He said wrong, 70 hours a week.

00:15:37.711 --> 00:15:40.367
And by the way, there's
almost no real vacation.

00:15:40.367 --> 00:15:41.872
When you take the cell phone with you,

00:15:41.872 --> 00:15:43.400
when you take the computer with you,

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:45.057
when you go on that vacation

00:15:45.057 --> 00:15:47.179
you're constantly wired in.

00:15:47.179 --> 00:15:49.546
There's almost no real vacation.

00:15:49.546 --> 00:15:52.494
Very important point
for young people today.

00:15:52.494 --> 00:15:54.270
Very important point.

00:15:54.270 --> 00:15:56.951
If you can work 35 or 40 hours a week

00:15:56.951 --> 00:15:59.012
and take five weeks of real vacation

00:15:59.012 --> 00:16:00.873
and you don't love what you do,

00:16:00.873 --> 00:16:02.945
it's not so bad,

00:16:02.945 --> 00:16:04.249
it's not so bad.

00:16:04.249 --> 00:16:07.010
Kind of like a half time job anyway.

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:09.579
If you're working 70 hours a week

00:16:09.579 --> 00:16:11.731
and you get no weeks real vacation

00:16:11.731 --> 00:16:14.021
and you do not love what you do

00:16:14.021 --> 00:16:16.940
you're living in a bad
place called New Age

00:16:16.940 --> 00:16:18.933
professional hell.

00:16:18.933 --> 00:16:21.983
And too many people
are living there today.

00:16:21.983 --> 00:16:24.876
Very important challenge
for people at all ages

00:16:24.876 --> 00:16:26.710
especially young people

00:16:26.710 --> 00:16:28.551
realize you're probably going to have

00:16:28.551 --> 00:16:29.937
to commit to your job.

00:16:29.937 --> 00:16:31.235
Realize you're probably going to be

00:16:31.235 --> 00:16:32.620
working many hours.

00:16:32.620 --> 00:16:34.673
Realize your occupation is going to be

00:16:34.673 --> 00:16:36.541
a large part of your life.

00:16:36.541 --> 00:16:38.323
Go back to that great question,

00:16:38.323 --> 00:16:40.519
do you love what you do?

00:16:40.519 --> 00:16:42.855
And to the degree you
can answer that question

00:16:42.855 --> 00:16:44.758
yes, you're probably going to be a winner

00:16:44.758 --> 00:16:46.142
in the game of life.

00:16:46.142 --> 00:16:48.316
To the gray you answer that question no

00:16:48.316 --> 00:16:49.949
you're going to have some problems.

00:16:49.949 --> 00:16:52.034
A classic problem of
smart successful people

00:16:52.034 --> 00:16:53.784
adding too much value

00:16:55.065 --> 00:16:56.058
what does this mean?

00:16:56.058 --> 00:16:58.145
I am young, smart, enthusiastic.

00:16:58.145 --> 00:17:00.144
I report to you I come
to you with an idea.

00:17:00.144 --> 00:17:01.502
You think it's a great idea.

00:17:01.502 --> 00:17:05.250
Rather than saying great
idea our tendency is to say,

00:17:05.250 --> 00:17:06.890
"That's a nice idea.

00:17:06.890 --> 00:17:09.473
"Why don't you add this to it?"

00:17:10.395 --> 00:17:13.849
The quality of the idea
may now go up five percent.

00:17:13.849 --> 00:17:15.537
My commitment to its execution

00:17:15.537 --> 00:17:17.251
may now go down 50%.

00:17:17.251 --> 00:17:18.672
It's no longer my idea.

00:17:18.672 --> 00:17:20.972
Incredibly hard for
smart successful people

00:17:20.972 --> 00:17:24.567
not to constantly go
through life adding value.

00:17:24.567 --> 00:17:26.448
Have any of you been
trained as scientists or

00:17:26.448 --> 00:17:28.220
engineers before?

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:30.811
Oh this is a particularly bad disease

00:17:30.811 --> 00:17:32.326
for this group okay.

00:17:32.326 --> 00:17:35.765
We get so wrapped up on improving
the quality five percent

00:17:35.765 --> 00:17:37.659
we may damage the commitment by 50%.

00:17:37.659 --> 00:17:39.349
And by the way, most the
time we're adding too

00:17:39.349 --> 00:17:41.824
much value what are we really doing

00:17:41.824 --> 00:17:44.741
telling the world how smart we are.

00:17:45.583 --> 00:17:48.018
It's incredibly hard for
smart successful people

00:17:48.018 --> 00:17:50.846
not to constantly go through
life telling the world

00:17:50.846 --> 00:17:51.829
how smart we are.

00:17:51.829 --> 00:17:55.118
Does anyone in the room have a PhD?

00:17:55.118 --> 00:17:57.971
Yes a particular disease
for people with a PhD

00:17:57.971 --> 00:17:58.865
right yeah yeah.

00:17:58.865 --> 00:18:00.996
Telling the world how smart we are.

00:18:00.996 --> 00:18:03.171
I have done programs myself

00:18:03.171 --> 00:18:06.521
called creating a great rest of your life

00:18:06.521 --> 00:18:09.958
in this very room with the many CEOs.

00:18:09.958 --> 00:18:11.662
My friend Alan Mulally CEO Ford has

00:18:11.662 --> 00:18:13.788
been in this room, Mike
Duke Seal of Walmart

00:18:13.788 --> 00:18:15.420
has been in this room.

00:18:15.420 --> 00:18:18.004
Many many top leaders
have been in this room.

00:18:18.004 --> 00:18:19.563
And we've talked about what are you

00:18:19.563 --> 00:18:20.396
going to do now?

00:18:20.396 --> 00:18:22.520
These are typically people my age

00:18:22.520 --> 00:18:24.341
older people and 60s

00:18:24.341 --> 00:18:26.994
maybe early 70s and talking about

00:18:26.994 --> 00:18:28.945
what are you going to do
with the rest of your life?

00:18:28.945 --> 00:18:31.995
And really what I've learned
is the concept of playing

00:18:31.995 --> 00:18:34.425
crappy golf with old
men at the Country Club

00:18:34.425 --> 00:18:36.665
while eating chicken salad sandwiches

00:18:36.665 --> 00:18:38.537
and discussing gallbladder surgery

00:18:38.537 --> 00:18:41.628
may not be quite as
wonderful as it sounds.

00:18:41.628 --> 00:18:43.876
Especially if you've
had a meaningful life.

00:18:43.876 --> 00:18:46.992
And we talk about what
is what matters in life

00:18:46.992 --> 00:18:49.262
really what matters and waht we've come up

00:18:49.262 --> 00:18:50.386
with a couple of things.

00:18:50.386 --> 00:18:52.688
One is you have to be
happy at what you're doing

00:18:52.688 --> 00:18:54.286
you have to love the process
of what you're doing.

00:18:54.286 --> 00:18:55.933
You don't want to be a victim or a martyr.

00:18:55.933 --> 00:18:58.051
And you also need to find meaning.

00:18:58.051 --> 00:19:00.415
You can't just go on
cruises and play bad golf.

00:19:00.415 --> 00:19:02.828
You have to do something
that matters in life.

00:19:02.828 --> 00:19:06.837
And so I think it is very
important at every stage of life

00:19:06.837 --> 00:19:08.922
we go back and look at what's important,

00:19:08.922 --> 00:19:11.414
what matters, and what do I want to be?

00:19:11.414 --> 00:19:14.509
We spend so little time
thinking about this.

00:19:14.509 --> 00:19:16.679
One of my good coaching clients who was

00:19:16.679 --> 00:19:18.298
going to retire in six months

00:19:18.298 --> 00:19:20.611
he was a CEO of a huge company.

00:19:20.611 --> 00:19:22.943
And I said if you knew the
company we're going to change

00:19:22.943 --> 00:19:25.828
in six months totally
different customers, everything

00:19:25.828 --> 00:19:27.238
would you plan for it?

00:19:27.238 --> 00:19:28.770
He said, "Of course I would
have be a responsible person

00:19:28.770 --> 00:19:29.845
"I'm the CEO."

00:19:29.845 --> 00:19:31.785
"So your life is going
to change in six months

00:19:31.785 --> 00:19:32.985
"totally change.

00:19:32.985 --> 00:19:34.997
"Everything's going to be different.

00:19:34.997 --> 00:19:36.882
"How much time have you
spent planning for that?"

00:19:36.882 --> 00:19:39.175
You know what he said, "None."

00:19:39.175 --> 00:19:40.647
Big mistake.
- Right.

00:19:40.647 --> 00:19:42.186
- Big big mistake.

00:19:42.186 --> 00:19:44.344
So I think it's very important as we go

00:19:44.344 --> 00:19:47.431
through various stages of life to reflect

00:19:47.431 --> 00:19:49.622
and think back what matters in life,

00:19:49.622 --> 00:19:50.888
what's important?

00:19:50.888 --> 00:19:53.202
And I don't feel any of us are too young

00:19:53.202 --> 00:19:55.822
or old to kind of begin this process.

00:19:55.822 --> 00:19:57.822
Creating a new identity.

00:19:59.165 --> 00:20:01.868
How can we become someone different?

00:20:01.868 --> 00:20:04.817
How can we create a new identity?

00:20:04.817 --> 00:20:06.782
Now the final element of our identity

00:20:06.782 --> 00:20:08.232
if you look at our model it's called

00:20:08.232 --> 00:20:09.452
our created identity.

00:20:09.452 --> 00:20:12.588
That's that new person that we can create.

00:20:12.588 --> 00:20:15.557
That's the future that
we create for ourselves.

00:20:15.557 --> 00:20:17.979
And in looking at this element of identity

00:20:17.979 --> 00:20:19.392
I'm going to give you an example

00:20:19.392 --> 00:20:21.063
from a famous rock star.

00:20:21.063 --> 00:20:23.556
One night I had the
opportunity of having dinner

00:20:23.556 --> 00:20:25.945
with the famous rock and roll star Bono.

00:20:25.945 --> 00:20:28.612
And I didn't know any
of his music too much

00:20:28.612 --> 00:20:31.314
because it was recorded after 1975.

00:20:31.314 --> 00:20:33.298
We had a wonderful dinner though

00:20:33.298 --> 00:20:35.240
and he talked about not his music

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:37.572
he talked about a lot about his identity.

00:20:37.572 --> 00:20:41.258
And the first part of his
identity was kind of regular guy.

00:20:41.258 --> 00:20:44.082
Not pompous or pretentious,
very much a regular person.

00:20:44.082 --> 00:20:45.802
And you know he impressed
me as being very much

00:20:45.802 --> 00:20:47.738
a regular guy, didn't put on airs

00:20:47.738 --> 00:20:50.850
just seemed to be a real real person.

00:20:50.850 --> 00:20:52.532
He used the F-word about every third word

00:20:52.532 --> 00:20:54.343
when we were talking and he's a nice man.

00:20:54.343 --> 00:20:55.995
He apologized he said, "You know sir,

00:20:55.995 --> 00:20:58.638
"I probably shouldn't use
such bad language around you."

00:20:58.638 --> 00:21:01.534
I said, "Excuse me I'm from
Valley Station, Kentucky.

00:21:01.534 --> 00:21:04.252
"I thought the F-word was the adjective

00:21:04.252 --> 00:21:06.742
"that preceded all nouns."

00:21:06.742 --> 00:21:10.443
So I said, "This really
doesn't bother me that much."

00:21:10.443 --> 00:21:12.295
Well after a regular guy

00:21:12.295 --> 00:21:14.743
the next part of his identity
was rock and roll fan.

00:21:14.743 --> 00:21:16.533
He's still a fan of
music and he talked about

00:21:16.533 --> 00:21:19.691
the group's he loved and how
they influenced his life.

00:21:19.691 --> 00:21:21.512
Then he became a musician.

00:21:21.512 --> 00:21:22.345
And he's still a musician.

00:21:22.345 --> 00:21:24.260
He talked about how he loved making music

00:21:24.260 --> 00:21:26.597
and not just for money how he had

00:21:26.597 --> 00:21:28.184
times with his family and they'd all

00:21:28.184 --> 00:21:29.759
sing and make music together and how

00:21:29.759 --> 00:21:31.958
much he enjoyed that.

00:21:31.958 --> 00:21:33.445
Then he became a rock
star and he talked about

00:21:33.445 --> 00:21:34.875
what it's like to be a rock star.

00:21:34.875 --> 00:21:36.507
And he wasn't bragging or showing off

00:21:36.507 --> 00:21:37.985
that's who he is, he's a rock star.

00:21:37.985 --> 00:21:40.058
He talked about the pluses and minuses

00:21:40.058 --> 00:21:41.485
of that life.

00:21:41.485 --> 00:21:43.453
And now he's a humanitarian.

00:21:43.453 --> 00:21:45.694
He is devoting a whole lot of his life

00:21:45.694 --> 00:21:47.583
to trying to help others.

00:21:47.583 --> 00:21:50.794
And he made an interesting point

00:21:50.794 --> 00:21:52.238
not so easy.

00:21:52.238 --> 00:21:54.100
Everybody didn't support him in trying

00:21:54.100 --> 00:21:55.756
to be a humanitarian.

00:21:55.756 --> 00:21:58.458
Some people were more into
like well who is this guy

00:21:58.458 --> 00:22:01.174
he's shallow, he's plastic some rock star.

00:22:01.174 --> 00:22:03.992
What's he trying to be
some kind of humanitarian?

00:22:03.992 --> 00:22:06.222
And to his credit you know
what he said to himself?

00:22:06.222 --> 00:22:07.218
"Forget that.

00:22:07.218 --> 00:22:08.051
"If I want to help people I
shouldn't have to apologize

00:22:08.051 --> 00:22:10.334
"or be ashamed of it."

00:22:10.334 --> 00:22:12.717
And he is devoting a
huge amount of his life

00:22:12.717 --> 00:22:14.423
right now to helping others.

00:22:14.423 --> 00:22:16.834
Well to me God bless him.

00:22:16.834 --> 00:22:19.803
He has created a new identity for himself

00:22:19.803 --> 00:22:23.272
without being a hypocrite
and without being a phony.

00:22:23.272 --> 00:22:25.169
Final point for you

00:22:25.169 --> 00:22:27.027
challenge yourself.

00:22:27.027 --> 00:22:30.246
Think about what is my identity now?

00:22:30.246 --> 00:22:33.221
Second, where did that identity come from?

00:22:33.221 --> 00:22:34.545
My memories?

00:22:34.545 --> 00:22:35.930
Feedback from others?

00:22:35.930 --> 00:22:37.502
Programming?

00:22:37.502 --> 00:22:41.309
What is a new person I want to create?

00:22:41.309 --> 00:22:43.602
How can I create a new identity,

00:22:43.602 --> 00:22:46.443
a different person, a better person?

00:22:46.443 --> 00:22:48.432
A person who's not the same as a

00:22:48.432 --> 00:22:50.511
person who's there now

00:22:50.511 --> 00:22:51.830
without being a hypocrite

00:22:51.830 --> 00:22:53.321
without being a phony?

00:22:53.321 --> 00:22:55.071
Creating a better me.

00:22:57.968 --> 00:22:59.288
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:22:59.288 --> 00:23:02.284
I made this video because
Jeff Davis asked me to.

00:23:02.284 --> 00:23:03.494
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:23:03.494 --> 00:23:05.427
that you want me to profile next

00:23:05.427 --> 00:23:06.586
leave in the comments below

00:23:06.586 --> 00:23:07.575
and I'll see what I can do.

00:23:07.575 --> 00:23:09.378
I'd also love to know what of the rules

00:23:09.378 --> 00:23:10.630
had the biggest impact on you?

00:23:10.630 --> 00:23:13.275
What did Marshall say that
really resonated with you?

00:23:13.275 --> 00:23:15.136
What's the single biggest lesson that you

00:23:15.136 --> 00:23:16.046
took from this video?

00:23:16.046 --> 00:23:17.143
Leave it in the comments below

00:23:17.143 --> 00:23:18.615
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:23:18.615 --> 00:23:20.418
So thank you guys so much for watching.

00:23:20.418 --> 00:23:22.617
I believe in you, I hope you continue to

00:23:22.617 --> 00:23:26.262
believe in yourself and
whatever your one word is.

00:23:26.262 --> 00:23:28.679
Much love, I'll see you soon.

00:23:34.717 --> 00:23:36.877
- The first disease of successful people

00:23:36.877 --> 00:23:39.585
I can see a running rampant in this room

00:23:39.585 --> 00:23:41.342
what is this problem called?

00:23:41.342 --> 00:23:43.033
Winning too much.

00:23:43.033 --> 00:23:44.649
Now what does that mean if it is important

00:23:44.649 --> 00:23:46.535
we want to win.

00:23:46.535 --> 00:23:48.706
Meaningful we want to win.

00:23:48.706 --> 00:23:50.838
Critical we want to win.

00:23:50.838 --> 00:23:53.044
Trivial we want to win.

00:23:53.044 --> 00:23:55.959
Not worth it we want to win anyway.

00:23:55.959 --> 00:23:58.205
(laughing)
We like winning.

00:23:58.205 --> 00:23:59.757
I'm going to give you a case study that

00:23:59.757 --> 00:24:02.986
75% of my successful clients fail.

00:24:02.986 --> 00:24:05.412
Yes I will predict most of you will fail

00:24:05.412 --> 00:24:06.728
this case study.

00:24:06.728 --> 00:24:09.890
And when I say fail they fail themselves.

00:24:09.890 --> 00:24:12.536
They say what I would do is the opposite

00:24:12.536 --> 00:24:14.501
of what I know I should do.

00:24:14.501 --> 00:24:16.393
Are you ready?

00:24:16.393 --> 00:24:20.086
You want to go to dinner at restaurant X.

00:24:20.086 --> 00:24:21.447
Your husband, wife, partner, friend or

00:24:21.447 --> 00:24:23.456
significant other wants to go to dinner

00:24:23.456 --> 00:24:25.310
at restaurant Y.

00:24:25.310 --> 00:24:27.066
You have heated argument.

00:24:27.066 --> 00:24:28.862
You go to restaurant Y.

00:24:28.862 --> 00:24:30.691
This was not your choice.

00:24:30.691 --> 00:24:34.145
The food tastes awful,
the service is terrible.

00:24:34.145 --> 00:24:36.254
Option A, critique the food.

00:24:36.254 --> 00:24:38.488
Point out our partner was wrong.

00:24:38.488 --> 00:24:39.874
This mistake could have been avoided

00:24:39.874 --> 00:24:41.257
if only they would have listened

00:24:41.257 --> 00:24:42.757
to me me me me me.

00:24:43.665 --> 00:24:45.165
Option B, shut up.

00:24:46.059 --> 00:24:48.995
Eat the stupid food, try to enjoy it

00:24:48.995 --> 00:24:51.271
and have a nice evening.

00:24:51.271 --> 00:24:53.131
What would I do?

00:24:53.131 --> 00:24:54.708
What should I do?

00:24:54.708 --> 00:24:57.128
75% of my clients what would I do,

00:24:57.128 --> 00:24:58.503
critique the food.

00:24:58.503 --> 00:25:01.127
What should I do, shut up.

00:25:01.127 --> 00:25:02.439
How many people in the room

00:25:02.439 --> 00:25:03.962
have ever critiqued the food before?

00:25:03.962 --> 00:25:05.024
Raise your hands food critiquers, yes

00:25:05.024 --> 00:25:06.837
a room filled with food critiquers.

00:25:06.837 --> 00:25:09.159
Was this a smart thing or a stupid thing?

00:25:09.159 --> 00:25:10.921
- [Man] Stupid.
- Stupid.

00:25:10.921 --> 00:25:11.777
(laughing)

00:25:11.777 --> 00:25:13.920
And as stupid as it was stupid.

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:15.283
I'm going to give you an example now

00:25:15.283 --> 00:25:17.932
that is so hideously stupid

00:25:17.932 --> 00:25:20.384
it will make that one pale by comparison.

00:25:20.384 --> 00:25:21.988
And yes I will predict
that most of you have

00:25:21.988 --> 00:25:23.991
done this, are you ready?

00:25:23.991 --> 00:25:26.894
You have a hard day at work a hard day.

00:25:26.894 --> 00:25:29.159
You come home, your husband, wife, friend

00:25:29.159 --> 00:25:30.463
or partner is there.

00:25:30.463 --> 00:25:32.658
And the other person says, "I had such

00:25:32.658 --> 00:25:35.871
"a hard day today, I
had such a tough day."

00:25:35.871 --> 00:25:38.301
And we reply, "You had a hard day?

00:25:38.301 --> 00:25:39.797
"You had a hard day?

00:25:39.797 --> 00:25:42.277
"Do you have any idea what
I had to put up with today?

00:25:42.277 --> 00:25:44.699
"Do you think you had a hard day?"

00:25:44.699 --> 00:25:46.117
We are so competitive

00:25:46.117 --> 00:25:48.302
we have to prove we are more miserable

00:25:48.302 --> 00:25:49.658
than a person lives with us.

00:25:49.658 --> 00:25:51.227
(laughing)

00:25:51.227 --> 00:25:52.060
I give this example in one class the guy

00:25:52.060 --> 00:25:54.242
in the back raised his hand.

00:25:54.242 --> 00:25:56.434
He said, "I did that last week."

00:25:56.434 --> 00:25:58.179
I ask him I say, "What happened?"

00:25:58.179 --> 00:25:59.960
He said, "My wife looked at me, she said,

00:25:59.960 --> 00:26:02.694
""Honey, you just think
you have had a hard day

00:26:02.694 --> 00:26:04.094
"it's not over.""

00:26:04.094 --> 00:26:06.826
(laughing)

00:26:06.826 --> 00:26:08.765
I'd like to give you two examples

00:26:08.765 --> 00:26:11.250
of the value of help structure

00:26:11.250 --> 00:26:12.791
and direction.

00:26:12.791 --> 00:26:16.658
A very positive example
and a negative example.

00:26:16.658 --> 00:26:18.648
First, the negative.

00:26:18.648 --> 00:26:21.825
Dr. Atul Gawande from
Harvard Medical School

00:26:21.825 --> 00:26:25.466
wrote a great book called
The Checklist Manifesto.

00:26:25.466 --> 00:26:28.225
In his book Dr. Gawande shares some

00:26:28.225 --> 00:26:30.137
pretty sobering research.

00:26:30.137 --> 00:26:32.670
If you go in for a surgery and the nurse

00:26:32.670 --> 00:26:35.591
asks the doctor a series
of very simple questions

00:26:35.591 --> 00:26:37.624
from a checklist before the surgery

00:26:37.624 --> 00:26:40.441
the odds on unneeded infection plummet

00:26:40.441 --> 00:26:42.741
and the death rate because
unnecessary infection

00:26:42.741 --> 00:26:44.741
is dramatically reduced.

00:26:45.610 --> 00:26:49.183
The huge majority of
hospitals around the world

00:26:49.183 --> 00:26:52.622
do not allow the nurse to
ask the doctor the questions.

00:26:52.622 --> 00:26:55.453
Why doesn't the doctor
want the nurse asking

00:26:55.453 --> 00:26:58.606
them those questions from the checklist?

00:26:58.606 --> 00:27:00.207
Ego.

00:27:00.207 --> 00:27:02.885
The doctor is too proud to admit

00:27:02.885 --> 00:27:04.630
they need that help.

00:27:04.630 --> 00:27:06.665
Too proud to get that day to day

00:27:06.665 --> 00:27:08.082
to day structure.

00:27:08.975 --> 00:27:11.283
And they let their ego and pride become

00:27:11.283 --> 00:27:13.846
more important than their patients.

00:27:13.846 --> 00:27:16.442
According to Dr. Gawande
more people have died

00:27:16.442 --> 00:27:18.740
because of the egos of surgeons

00:27:18.740 --> 00:27:20.739
than died in the Vietnam
War, the Afghan war

00:27:20.739 --> 00:27:23.008
and the Iraqi war combined.

00:27:23.008 --> 00:27:25.920
What is the first question
the nurse asks the doctor?

00:27:25.920 --> 00:27:27.560
Did you wash your hands?

00:27:27.560 --> 00:27:29.422
Did you wash your hands?

00:27:29.422 --> 00:27:30.600
Too much ego.

00:27:30.600 --> 00:27:32.717
That's the negative case study.

00:27:32.717 --> 00:27:34.609
Now the positive case study

00:27:34.609 --> 00:27:37.102
every year I trained the
Admirals for nine years

00:27:37.102 --> 00:27:39.437
in the United States Navy.

00:27:39.437 --> 00:27:41.352
And I love working with the Navy I

00:27:41.352 --> 00:27:43.300
I don't bill them any money
because they're billing

00:27:43.300 --> 00:27:46.320
bureaucracy is such a
phenomenal pain in the butt.

00:27:46.320 --> 00:27:47.984
I said we'll both make more money

00:27:47.984 --> 00:27:50.075
if you pay me nothing, don't pay me.

00:27:50.075 --> 00:27:51.664
They give me treats.

00:27:51.664 --> 00:27:54.808
Afew years ago my treat
day in a nuclear submarine

00:27:54.808 --> 00:27:56.624
me and eight other old
men got to go out on a

00:27:56.624 --> 00:27:59.377
nuclear sub diving down under the waters

00:27:59.377 --> 00:28:02.856
for eight hours and
pretending to torpedo things.

00:28:02.856 --> 00:28:05.031
Ah we had a great old time.

00:28:05.031 --> 00:28:07.695
A couple years later I
got an even better treat

00:28:07.695 --> 00:28:09.606
where did we get to go?

00:28:09.606 --> 00:28:11.856
Highway to the danger zone.

00:28:14.147 --> 00:28:16.867
Have you seen that movie Top Gun?

00:28:16.867 --> 00:28:20.140
I got to spend 95 minutes
on a United States Navy

00:28:20.140 --> 00:28:22.286
Top Gun fighter jet.

00:28:22.286 --> 00:28:26.486
A 6 G's oxygen flying
upside down doing tricks.

00:28:26.486 --> 00:28:28.968
I got to fly the plane
myself for 10 minutes.

00:28:28.968 --> 00:28:32.801
And I am very proud to
say I did not throw up.

00:28:33.970 --> 00:28:37.265
Now before I got on the
plane I noticed something.

00:28:37.265 --> 00:28:40.528
This kid is asking my
host Admiral Mark Watney

00:28:40.528 --> 00:28:43.653
a series of simple
questions from a checklist.

00:28:43.653 --> 00:28:45.620
These weren't tricky questions.

00:28:45.620 --> 00:28:48.854
First question for the doctor,
did you wash your hands?

00:28:48.854 --> 00:28:50.733
First question for the Admiral is,

00:28:50.733 --> 00:28:52.744
how much fuel they have.

00:28:52.744 --> 00:28:55.021
I said, "Sir this is puzzling.

00:28:55.021 --> 00:28:56.804
"The doctor don't like the nurse

00:28:56.804 --> 00:28:57.952
"she asking him the question because

00:28:57.952 --> 00:28:59.415
"their ego iss too big."

00:28:59.415 --> 00:29:01.593
Now I said, "No offense sir

00:29:01.593 --> 00:29:04.805
"nobody in the world has a much bigger ego

00:29:04.805 --> 00:29:07.234
"than a United States Navy Top Gun

00:29:07.234 --> 00:29:09.845
"fighter jet pilot who becomes an admiral.

00:29:09.845 --> 00:29:11.654
"You've probably got
one of the biggest egos

00:29:11.654 --> 00:29:13.936
"in the world yet you do not object

00:29:13.936 --> 00:29:16.584
"to this kid asking you the questions."

00:29:16.584 --> 00:29:19.301
I said, "Sir, what's the difference?"

00:29:19.301 --> 00:29:22.522
He said, "Marshall
there's a huge difference.

00:29:22.522 --> 00:29:25.976
"If that operation crashes, you die.

00:29:25.976 --> 00:29:28.951
"If that plane crashes, I die."

00:29:28.951 --> 00:29:31.198
He said, "You put a gun to
that surgeons head and say,

00:29:31.198 --> 00:29:33.395
""This patient dies of
an unneeded infection

00:29:33.395 --> 00:29:35.145
"we're going to blow your brains out"

00:29:35.145 --> 00:29:37.507
"they'd probably be asking
those questions twice."

00:29:37.507 --> 00:29:40.717
Well how important is ego
and how important is pride?

00:29:40.717 --> 00:29:43.791
Every day we make it more
important than our health.

00:29:43.791 --> 00:29:46.248
than our safety, than the people we love.

00:29:46.248 --> 00:29:49.738
Once we get over that ego and pride.

00:29:49.738 --> 00:29:52.926
Once we get over that macho nonsense of

00:29:52.926 --> 00:29:56.445
ahh I can do it on my own,
I don't need that checklist,

00:29:56.445 --> 00:29:58.545
I'm above all that stuff.

00:29:58.545 --> 00:30:01.056
Once we get over that ridiculous ego

00:30:01.056 --> 00:30:04.000
that surgery is likely to go a lot better.

00:30:04.000 --> 00:30:07.899
That plane flight is likely
to be more successful

00:30:07.899 --> 00:30:10.092
and we are more likely
to become the person

00:30:10.092 --> 00:30:11.905
in our lives that we want to become.

00:30:11.905 --> 00:30:13.878
I'm now going to teach you a process

00:30:13.878 --> 00:30:17.623
that takes about two minutes a day,

00:30:17.623 --> 00:30:19.706
costs absolutely nothing.

00:30:21.043 --> 00:30:25.729
It's going to help you get
better at almost anything.

00:30:25.729 --> 00:30:27.898
You might be a little skeptical right now.

00:30:27.898 --> 00:30:30.589
Two minutes a day, cost nothing,

00:30:30.589 --> 00:30:33.308
help me get better at almost anything.

00:30:33.308 --> 00:30:37.219
Well that sounds too good to be true.

00:30:37.219 --> 00:30:39.193
I'm going to make a second prediction

00:30:39.193 --> 00:30:41.816
half of the people I teach this to

00:30:41.816 --> 00:30:44.594
you're going to quit doing
this within two weeks.

00:30:44.594 --> 00:30:48.823
And you will not quit doing
it because it does not work.

00:30:48.823 --> 00:30:51.865
You will quit doing it
because it does work.

00:30:51.865 --> 00:30:53.863
You see what I'm going to teach you next

00:30:53.863 --> 00:30:56.613
is incredibly easy to understand.

00:30:58.249 --> 00:31:01.296
It's just incredibly difficult to do.

00:31:01.296 --> 00:31:04.092
This is called the daily question process.

00:31:04.092 --> 00:31:05.906
Now how does this work?

00:31:05.906 --> 00:31:08.099
Here's what I want you to do.

00:31:08.099 --> 00:31:11.222
I want you to get out
an Excel spreadsheet.

00:31:11.222 --> 00:31:13.291
On one column I want you to write a list

00:31:13.291 --> 00:31:15.854
of questions, questions that represent

00:31:15.854 --> 00:31:18.421
what is most important in your life.

00:31:18.421 --> 00:31:21.698
Could be friends, family, colleagues.

00:31:21.698 --> 00:31:24.315
Every question must be
answered with a yes, a no

00:31:24.315 --> 00:31:25.372
or a number.

00:31:25.372 --> 00:31:27.149
A yes is recorded as a one.

00:31:27.149 --> 00:31:28.789
A no is recorded as a zero.

00:31:28.789 --> 00:31:31.292
Or a number, whatever that number is.

00:31:31.292 --> 00:31:33.963
And then seven boxes across
one for every day of the

00:31:33.963 --> 00:31:36.249
week Monday Tuesday Wednesday Thursday

00:31:36.249 --> 00:31:38.225
Friday Saturday Sunday.

00:31:38.225 --> 00:31:41.016
Fill it out every day and
at the end of the week

00:31:41.016 --> 00:31:42.766
that Excel spreadsheet will give you

00:31:42.766 --> 00:31:44.459
a little report card.

00:31:44.459 --> 00:31:48.583
I've been doing this for
years and let me warn you

00:31:48.583 --> 00:31:50.906
that report card at the end of the week

00:31:50.906 --> 00:31:53.715
might not be quite as pretty

00:31:53.715 --> 00:31:55.373
as that corporate values plaque you got

00:31:55.373 --> 00:31:57.446
stuck up there on the wall.

00:31:57.446 --> 00:31:58.962
When you do this every day

00:31:58.962 --> 00:32:01.561
you know what you learn very quickly?

00:32:01.561 --> 00:32:03.728
Life is real easy to talk.

00:32:05.412 --> 00:32:07.995
Life is real difficult to live.

00:32:08.894 --> 00:32:10.287
Now I'm going to share some

00:32:10.287 --> 00:32:12.390
of my daily questions.

00:32:12.390 --> 00:32:14.897
Mine are not intended to be yours.

00:32:14.897 --> 00:32:16.931
The idea of this exercise is you write

00:32:16.931 --> 00:32:19.524
your own questions every day.

00:32:19.524 --> 00:32:23.830
For example, what is one
of my questions every day?

00:32:23.830 --> 00:32:27.380
How many times yesterday
did you try to prove

00:32:27.380 --> 00:32:31.130
that you were right when
it was not worth it?

00:32:32.282 --> 00:32:35.604
I've almost never scored
a zero in the whole life.

00:32:35.604 --> 00:32:37.345
Kind of hard for that old professor

00:32:37.345 --> 00:32:39.762
not to be right all the time.

00:32:40.618 --> 00:32:42.710
How many angry or
destructive comments did you

00:32:42.710 --> 00:32:45.414
make about people yesterday?

00:32:45.414 --> 00:32:47.917
Well if I want to be a good person

00:32:47.917 --> 00:32:50.952
why am I stabbing people in the back?

00:32:50.952 --> 00:32:52.861
How many minutes did you walk?

00:32:52.861 --> 00:32:54.180
How many pushups did you do?

00:32:54.180 --> 00:32:55.828
How many sit-ups did you do?

00:32:55.828 --> 00:32:58.761
Did you say or do something
nice for your wife?

00:32:58.761 --> 00:33:00.924
Did you say or do something
nice for your son,

00:33:00.924 --> 00:33:05.091
for your daughter, for your
son-in-law, for your grandkids?

00:33:05.947 --> 00:33:08.475
How many minutes did you write?

00:33:08.475 --> 00:33:10.868
Well a friend of mine Jim
Morris has been doing this for

00:33:10.868 --> 00:33:13.653
years and when he began doing it one of

00:33:13.653 --> 00:33:15.557
his daily questions was,

00:33:15.557 --> 00:33:19.928
are you currently updated on
your physical examination?

00:33:19.928 --> 00:33:21.758
The first 90 days he did this he said

00:33:21.758 --> 00:33:23.791
no every day for 90 days.

00:33:23.791 --> 00:33:24.624
No no no.

00:33:26.082 --> 00:33:29.582
After 90 days he said this is embarrassing

00:33:30.448 --> 00:33:32.878
it's embarrassing I got
to get the dumb exam

00:33:32.878 --> 00:33:35.412
or quit asking a question,
it's embarrassing.

00:33:35.412 --> 00:33:37.168
He got the dumb exam.

00:33:37.168 --> 00:33:38.976
What did the doctor say?

00:33:38.976 --> 00:33:40.640
"You have cancer."

00:33:40.640 --> 00:33:43.405
Now he's going to be fine,
that was many years ago.

00:33:43.405 --> 00:33:47.120
The doctor also said, "Had
you waited seven more months

00:33:47.120 --> 00:33:49.184
"you would be dead."

00:33:49.184 --> 00:33:51.735
He knew he should have
got a physical exam.

00:33:51.735 --> 00:33:53.397
He's not a stupid man.

00:33:53.397 --> 00:33:55.419
Chief Learning Officer
of three multi-billion

00:33:55.419 --> 00:33:57.520
dollar companies.

00:33:57.520 --> 00:34:01.642
He knew he should have done
it, but he didn't do it.

00:34:01.642 --> 00:34:05.641
In my classes I ask people,
"How many of you have put off

00:34:05.641 --> 00:34:07.940
"getting a physical exam
before and told yourself,

00:34:07.940 --> 00:34:10.130
""I'm getting that exam after I go on my

00:34:10.130 --> 00:34:11.716
"healthy foods diet.

00:34:11.716 --> 00:34:15.251
"I'm getting that exam after
I begin my exercise program.""

00:34:15.251 --> 00:34:17.612
Pretty much half the
class raises their hand.

00:34:17.612 --> 00:34:19.754
And then I say, "Did you really trick

00:34:19.754 --> 00:34:22.644
"the doctor with that strategy?"

00:34:22.644 --> 00:34:24.562
Well I was teaching at
the University of Michigan

00:34:24.562 --> 00:34:25.993
this guy raised his hand, I said,

00:34:25.993 --> 00:34:28.956
"Sir, did you trick the
doctor with that strategy?"

00:34:28.956 --> 00:34:32.646
He looked at me, he
goes, "I am the doctor."

00:34:32.646 --> 00:34:35.813
Well why don't we ask these questions?

00:34:37.382 --> 00:34:39.566
We're afraid of the answers.

00:34:39.566 --> 00:34:42.229
If you have the courage
to write down the behavior

00:34:42.229 --> 00:34:44.978
that's most important in your life

00:34:44.978 --> 00:34:47.931
and the discipline to do this every day

00:34:47.931 --> 00:34:49.893
you're going to get a lot better.

00:34:49.893 --> 00:34:53.580
Now I pay a woman named
Kate to call me on the

00:34:53.580 --> 00:34:56.502
phone every day, she called me today.

00:34:56.502 --> 00:34:58.075
She's going to call me tomorrow.

00:34:58.075 --> 00:34:59.368
She called me yesterday.

00:34:59.368 --> 00:35:01.920
She calls me every day.

00:35:01.920 --> 00:35:04.388
Every day she listens to
me read these questions

00:35:04.388 --> 00:35:06.651
that I wrote and provide the answers

00:35:06.651 --> 00:35:08.568
that I wrote every day.

00:35:10.288 --> 00:35:12.464
Now someone asked me,
"Why do you pay a woman

00:35:12.464 --> 00:35:14.528
"to call you on the phone every day?

00:35:14.528 --> 00:35:16.372
"Don't you know the theory about

00:35:16.372 --> 00:35:18.660
"how to change behavior?"

00:35:18.660 --> 00:35:21.878
"I wrote the theory about
how to change behavior.

00:35:21.878 --> 00:35:24.913
"That's why I pay a woman
to call me every day."

00:35:24.913 --> 00:35:27.497
I know how hard this is.

00:35:27.497 --> 00:35:30.111
I have over 12 million
frequent flier miles

00:35:30.111 --> 00:35:32.360
just on American Airlines.

00:35:32.360 --> 00:35:35.496
That's an excuse to be a bad father,

00:35:35.496 --> 00:35:38.635
a bad husband, out of shape.

00:35:38.635 --> 00:35:41.863
How many excuses do I get
with the 12 million miles?

00:35:41.863 --> 00:35:42.946
Many excuses.

00:35:43.997 --> 00:35:46.068
Excuses don't help.

00:35:46.068 --> 00:35:47.864
It's hard for me, it's hard for you,

00:35:47.864 --> 00:35:49.691
it's hard for all of us.

00:35:49.691 --> 00:35:51.296
Why do I pay a woman named Kate

00:35:51.296 --> 00:35:53.265
to call me on the phone every day?

00:35:53.265 --> 00:35:55.712
Just to listen to me
read questions I wrote

00:35:55.712 --> 00:35:57.994
and provide answers I wrote&gt;

00:35:57.994 --> 00:35:59.880
My name is Marshall Goldsmith

00:35:59.880 --> 00:36:02.622
and the world's top-ranked
executive coach.

00:36:02.622 --> 00:36:05.615
I am too cowardly to do this on my own

00:36:05.615 --> 00:36:08.560
and too undisciplined
to do this on my own.

00:36:08.560 --> 00:36:10.171
Why do I pay someone to call me

00:36:10.171 --> 00:36:11.566
on the phone every day?

00:36:11.566 --> 00:36:13.084
My name is Marshall Goldsmith

00:36:13.084 --> 00:36:15.531
I need help and it's okay.

00:36:15.531 --> 00:36:17.182
Now let me help you.

00:36:17.182 --> 00:36:20.311
Next step's get out
that Excel spreadsheet.

00:36:20.311 --> 00:36:22.280
Write down those questions.

00:36:22.280 --> 00:36:24.292
Every day test yourself.

00:36:24.292 --> 00:36:26.301
If you have the courage
and discipline to do

00:36:26.301 --> 00:36:27.942
this on your own every day

00:36:27.942 --> 00:36:30.109
knock yourself out, do it.

00:36:31.070 --> 00:36:33.356
If you notice that you're quitting,

00:36:33.356 --> 00:36:36.244
you make excuses like ahh

00:36:36.244 --> 00:36:39.726
I got too busy, didn't have time.

00:36:39.726 --> 00:36:40.883
It takes two minutes a day.

00:36:40.883 --> 00:36:42.243
You weren't too busy.

00:36:42.243 --> 00:36:43.243
I got bored.

00:36:44.290 --> 00:36:46.044
You didn't get bored.

00:36:46.044 --> 00:36:47.500
Why did you quit?

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:49.061
It's hard to face the reality

00:36:49.061 --> 00:36:51.103
of our own lives every day.

00:36:51.103 --> 00:36:53.735
If you have the courage
and discipline to do this

00:36:53.735 --> 00:36:55.036
by yourself, do it.

00:36:55.036 --> 00:36:56.908
If you do not have the
courage and discipline

00:36:56.908 --> 00:36:58.468
to do this by yourself,

00:36:58.468 --> 00:37:01.334
which most of us don't, like me,

00:37:01.334 --> 00:37:03.725
get somebody else to help you.

00:37:03.725 --> 00:37:06.051
And get over that shame
and pride thing and say,

00:37:06.051 --> 00:37:07.188
"Hey I need help."

00:37:07.188 --> 00:37:08.620
It's okay.

00:37:08.620 --> 00:37:09.793
It's okay.

00:37:09.793 --> 00:37:12.240
If you try this you're going to find out

00:37:12.240 --> 00:37:15.977
it will help you get better
at just about anything.

00:37:15.977 --> 00:37:18.100
And it doesn't take a lot of time

00:37:18.100 --> 00:37:19.537
it just takes a lot of courage

